"Uses unequal numeric bin widths while scaling heights so each bar area represents frequency or probability.\n\nUse when: Domain-defined intervals differ in width or long tails need coarser bins. Readers must compare frequencies without being misled by unequal intervals.\n\nAvoid when: The audience is likely to compare heights as raw counts despite clear labels.\n\nVisual grammar: Bar width encodes the actual numeric interval. Height encodes count divided by bin width, optionally divided again by sample size.\n\nBuild: Choose defensible numeric intervals and preserve their true widths. Compute frequency density separately for each interval. Draw adjoining rectangles and include a clear density axis and endpoint labels.\n\nCheck: Check density multiplied by width recovers each bin count or probability. Verify total bar area equals the declared count or one. State the treatment of open-ended terminal bins.\n\nPitfalls: Using raw counts for unequal-width bar heights distorts the distribution. Do not compress a wide tail interval into a narrow visual slot.\n\nExample: Summarize synthetic wait times using 0–5, 5–10, 10–30, and 30–60 minute bins with frequency-density heights.\n\nAlternatives: Histogram, ECDF, Density plot."
